import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { SliderHero } from "../../demos/slider/hero.rx"
import { SliderRange } from "../../demos/slider/range.rx"
import { SliderVertical } from "../../demos/slider/vertical.rx"
import { SliderControlled } from "../../demos/slider/controlled.rx"
import { SliderSaving } from "../../demos/slider/saving.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Slider" subtitle="A range input with any number of thumbs that you can style.">
    <Demo source="slider/hero"><SliderHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="slider/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      Each thumb is a positioned <code>{"<div>"}</code> around a visually hidden{" "}
      <code>{"<input type=\"range\">"}</code>. The native input is what takes focus, carries the slider's ARIA
      (with <code>aria-valuetext</code> from the formatted value) and submits the thumb's value with a form. A
      change the input reports on its own, from assistive technology or autofill, updates the slider too.
    </p>
    <p>
      The visible slider is not a native widget. A native range input has a single thumb and no way to show a
      filled range between two, and several thumbs on one track is something only the library does. So the
      library draws the track, places the indicator and the thumbs with inline styles, moves the nearest thumb to
      a press on the control and follows a drag, and handles the keys itself: the arrow keys step,{" "}
      <kbd>Shift</kbd> with an arrow and <kbd>Page Up</kbd>/<kbd>Page Down</kbd> take the large step, and{" "}
      <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd> go to the bounds. It also formats the values with{" "}
      <code>Intl.NumberFormat</code> and connects the slider to an enclosing Field.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        There is no Label part: put the slider in a <code>FieldRoot</code> with a <code>FieldLabel</code>, point{" "}
        <code>aria-labelledby</code> on the root at a label, or give each thumb an <code>aria-label</code>. Base
        UI's <code>thumbAlignment</code> and <code>thumbCollisionBehavior</code> don't exist: a thumb is centred
        on its value, so it overhangs the ends of the track by half its width, and in a range a dragged thumb pushes
        its neighbour along while a key stops it <code>minStepsBetweenValues</code> short.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="labelling">Labelling it</h3>
    <p>
      The first demo is in a <code>FieldRoot</code>, whose <code>FieldLabel</code> is a{" "}
      <code>{"<label>"}</code> for the first thumb's input and the root's <code>aria-labelledby</code>. Without a Field, an{" "}
      <code>aria-labelledby</code> on <code>SliderRoot</code> labels every thumb that has no{" "}
      <code>aria-label</code> of its own.
    </p>

    <h3 id="range">A range</h3>
    <p>
      Pass an array as <code>defaultValue</code> or <code>value</code> and put a <code>SliderThumb</code> for each
      entry, with its <code>index</code>. The indicator fills between the first and last thumbs.{" "}
      <code>getAriaLabel</code> names each thumb, and <code>minStepsBetweenValues</code> keeps them that many
      steps apart. <code>step</code> snaps the values to multiples from <code>min</code>; <code>largeStep</code> is
      what <kbd>Page Up</kbd>, <kbd>Page Down</kbd> and <kbd>Shift</kbd> with an arrow move. With a{" "}
      <code>format</code>, <code>SliderValue</code> shows the formatted values joined by an en dash.
    </p>
    <Demo source="slider/range"><SliderRange /></Demo>

    <h3 id="vertical">Vertical</h3>
    <p>
      With <code>orientation="vertical"</code> the bottom of the control is the minimum. The indicator then
      inherits the track's width instead of its height, so give the track a width.
    </p>
    <Demo source="slider/vertical"><SliderVertical /></Demo>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>value</code> and <code>onValueChange</code> to hold the value yourself. <code>onValueChange</code>{" "}
      is called on every change, many times during a drag; <code>onValueCommitted</code> only when a change is
      final: when the pointer is released, and on each key press. Both receive a number, or an array for a range,
      and a <code>details</code> object whose <code>reason</code> says what made the change.
    </p>
    <Demo source="slider/controlled"><SliderControlled /></Demo>

    <h3 id="saving">Saving to a server</h3>
    <p>
      A setting that saves when it changes needs only what is committed: save in{" "}
      <code>onValueCommitted</code>, and leave <code>onValueChange</code> out. The slider follows the pointer and
      the keys by itself while <code>value</code> stays where it was, and shows the owner's value again as soon as
      the owner's <code>onValueCommitted</code> has run. Write the handler as an action with a{" "}
      <code>guess</code>, and the thumb stays where it was released, says it is saving through{" "}
      <code>pending?</code>, and goes back by itself if the save fails. Ending a save with <code>refresh</code>{" "}
      asks the server what it holds, so when the slider is moved again while a save is out, the last value
      committed is what stays.
    </p>
    <Demo source="slider/saving"><SliderSaving /></Demo>
    <p>
      The slider calls its handlers in a change of their own, so a handler that waits for a server, or writes
      something a loading question reads, never holds the slider: it can be dragged again at once, and each key
      goes on from what the slider shows. Without a guess, the thumb goes back to the owner's value when the drag
      or key press is committed, and moves when the owner writes the new one.
    </p>
    <p>
      A slider disabled while its save is out (<code>disabled={"{"}pending? setting{"}"}</code>) loses focus, as
      every disabled control does, and gives it back to the thumb when it is enabled again if focus hasn't moved
      elsewhere. A <code>value</code> read from an answer that fails doesn't stop the slider: the failure is the
      app's to show, in an <code>errored</code> region around it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="forms">In a form</h3>
    <p>
      With a <code>name</code>, or inside a named <code>FieldRoot</code>, each thumb's range input submits its
      value under that name, so a range submits one entry per thumb.
    </p>

    <h3 id="styling">Styling the thumb</h3>
    <p>
      Focus is on the input inside the thumb, so style a keyboard focus ring with{" "}
      <code>.Thumb:has(:focus-visible)</code>. Every part carries <code>data-orientation</code>, and{" "}
      <code>data-dragging</code> while a pointer is pressing the control or dragging a thumb.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="slider" />
  </DocsPage>
}
